airs the choruses  as one and in fact in the early  renaissance  six chorus loots were common and indeed  this piece the locker rape vivant was  first published around 1595  for a six course loot so since choruses  are played as one  string really that's basically a guitar  all we have to do  is take the third string and tune it  down a half step and then we actually  have  the nominal tuning of a six course loot  and then the actual playing of the  instrument is quite similar  to the loot and we can indeed read  directly from  manuscripts from the renaissance period  which is what i'll be doing today  also i should mention my loots in g  but tuned at 4 15 at the moment meaning  that when i play  a g minor chord it actually sounds as a  g  flat minor chord everything's tuned down  a half step  so that means to have the same pitches  on my guitar i'm going to grab my capo  and put a capo on the second fret  tune that third string down  [Music]  a half step and now i have a loot  now for the showdown i'm going to use my  torres copy  which is a late 19th century style  guitar  made by sayers guitars again in loop  tuning  so i have a capo so i'm at the same  pitch i have gut strings  and i have no nails on the loot i also  have gut strings  on the first two courses and then i have  synthetic guts  for the rest on both instruments i'll be  using  late renaissance loop technique which is  basically  pinky down playing close to the bridge  and with a thumb out technique so this  position rather than the earlier loop  position  which is this it's possible that darwin  would have been playing  thumb out like this which is actually  not too dissimilar from the modern  classical guitar position  except for the pinky down and playing  without nails  one extra thing you should know is that  although i played both these instruments  i've been playing the guitar  a lot longer and so while i'm using loot  technique  on both of them and got strings i'm  quite a bit more  friendly 